+### Structured output
+
+`response_format: {"type":"json_object"}` constrains the response to a JSON object.
+`response_format: {"type":"json_schema","json_schema":{"schema":…,"name":…,"strict":…}}` constrains
+it to that schema, which is carried verbatim and compiled with a cache keyed by its text. `strict`
+is honored by applying the constraint either way, because constrained decoding is a superset of the
+guarantee the flag asks for. The equivalent fields are `text.format` on the Responses surface and
+`output_config.format` on the Messages surface; the Count Tokens route accepts and ignores the
+latter, since it never generates.
+
+The implemented schema subset covers every JSON value kind, `properties`/`required`/
+`additionalProperties`, `prefixItems`/`items`/`minItems`/`maxItems`, `enum`/`const`,
+`minLength`/`maxLength`, `pattern`, `anyOf`/`oneOf`/`allOf`, and `$ref` including recursive
+references. Constraints address the decoded string value, so `maxLength` counts code points and
+`pattern` matches unescaped text.
+
+A constrained request decodes one token per unit rather than drafting a speculative window. The mask
+the Engine hands the sampler describes exactly one position: the grammar advances one token at a
+time, while a speculative unit applies a single per-row sampling configuration to every column, so a
+draft column would be sampled against a state the constraint has already left behind. A unit that is
+not fully accepted cannot be reported either — the Engine's licensed-prefix contract admits a strict
+prefix only together with a finish — so the window is surrendered rather than the guarantee. Schema
+requests are the small auxiliary calls (titles, tool arguments), so the cost lands where the volume
+is lowest.
+
+`strict:true` on a tool declaration is honored the same way, under a constraint of its own. This
+template does not write a tool call's arguments as JSON: it writes
+`<tool_call><function=NAME><parameter=KEY>VALUE</parameter></function></tool_call>`, so the promise
+splits. The markup is constrained — a parameter may only name a declared property of that tool, no
+property is written twice, and `</function>` opens only once every `required` property is present —
+and each value is constrained by its property's schema, with a `string` argument read as raw text
+(the same `enum`/`const`/`pattern`/`minLength`/`maxLength` keywords apply) and every other type as
+the JSON literal it denotes. The constraint arms itself: the tool name is generated ahead of the
+arguments, so which schema governs them is only known from the bytes already emitted.
+
+A schema the Engine cannot enforce is a `400` with code `json_schema_invalid`, raised when the
+request is parsed rather than after it is admitted. Keywords that would narrow the language and are
+not implemented (`not`, `if`/`then`/`else`, `patternProperties`, `propertyNames`, `contains`,
+`uniqueItems`, `dependentSchemas`, `dependentRequired`, `unevaluatedProperties`,
+`unevaluatedItems`) are reported to the caller rather than silently ignored, so a request can be
+rejected instead of answered under a weaker constraint than it asked for.
